               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Great Zimbabwe\n\nOn this map, Africa has a small red dot that marks the approximate location of Great Zimbabwe (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "19934031-f0f5-4a1d-83c3-6391b5acaff8",
                     "text": "The National Museum and Monuments of Zimbabwe",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Shona people\n\nThe Bantu expansion led to about 500 different languages (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). One of the Bantu-speaking peoples is the Shona. Among their many achievements, they built what we now call Great Zimbabwe (<htcite2>), shown in **Figure 2**.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c5ab3b9d-0f70-4fa2-80b7-4fc381c47187",
                     "text": "Fortes-Lima, C.A., Burgarella, C., Hammarén, R. et al",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "8c10d904-305e-4a71-9ccf-28e6c83f7280",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "The Bantu People",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0c717575-d51a-464d-9be6-8ecfda771376",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Great Zimbabwe",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img class=\"imgcenter\" id=\"imgWALL\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgWALL', '0%')\"/><b>Figure 2</b>: Great Zimbabwe National Monument (<htcite0>). Photo by Erik Eschweiler-Dienerowitz, available on the UNESCO website (<htcite1>). Shared under the <a href=\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/licenses/6\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 IGO</a> license.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "19934031-f0f5-4a1d-83c3-6391b5acaff8",
                     "text": "The National Museum and Monuments of Zimbabwe",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b7d68d4b-67a9-4624-9ce6-9b0e2f01c50e",
                     "text": "UNESCO",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "12",
                        "day": "21"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "*History Tracers* already has a full text about Great Zimbabwe (<htcite0>). In this atlas entry, we focus on reflections about Shona trade with the Chinese during **Phase 3** (<htdate0>/<htdate1> - <htdate2>/<htdate3>) of ceramic finds in East Africa, as described by Dr. Bing Zhao (<htcite1>; <htcite2>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0c717575-d51a-464d-9be6-8ecfda771376",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Great Zimbabwe",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f7381734-81dc-4bef-8527-44317f40c4b4",
                     "text": "Zhao, B.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0c717575-d51a-464d-9be6-8ecfda771376",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Great Zimbabwe",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1220",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1250",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1430",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1450",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: On Trade</b><br /><br />What do you need in order to trade with another person?<br />And what if you want to trade with a whole other people?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Trade data\n\nIf you answered that money and goods are needed for trade, you are on the right track.\n\nStill, Dr. Bing Zhao warns us to be careful with money as evidence. Chinese copper coins are poor chronological markers: coins minted under one dynasty could still be used in foreign trade under later dynasties (<htcite0>). Even so, the article points out other clues worth noting (<htcite1>):\n- Chinese coins may have worked as a secondary currency in global trade.\n- The coins may show that Austronesians — peoples of East Asia, Oceania, and Madagascar with a shared seafaring background — were present in the Indian Ocean.\n\nAs for goods, Chinese ceramics found in East Africa fall into four distinct periods between <htdate0> and <htdate1> (<htcite2>; <htcite3>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f7381734-81dc-4bef-8527-44317f40c4b4",
                     "text": "Zhao, B.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f7381734-81dc-4bef-8527-44317f40c4b4",
                     "text": "Zhao, B.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f7381734-81dc-4bef-8527-44317f40c4b4",
                     "text": "Zhao, B.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0c717575-d51a-464d-9be6-8ecfda771376",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Great Zimbabwe",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "800",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1510",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### How did they work it out?\n\nSo far we know that East Africa had trade and that Chinese coins were there. We also know that the mix of goods changed over time. That suggests local people adapted to new products and to changes in their needs (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). This raises a question:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f7381734-81dc-4bef-8527-44317f40c4b4",
                     "text": "Zhao, B.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0c717575-d51a-464d-9be6-8ecfda771376",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Great Zimbabwe",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: How ...?</b><br /><br />Goods changed in size, and coins were present. But we have no known written records from the Shona — even though we know they spoke a language that came from a migration starting roughly between <htdate0> and <htdate1> (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>).<br /><br />Missing texts on one side did not stop trade. Did the Shona really never develop writing, or do we simply not know about it?<br /><br />To pay or barter, you must judge what something is worth. Counting systems have existed in Africa for over 30,000 years (<htcite3>). Could the Shona have used their own system to set values?<br /><br />If they had a number system, could it also have included zero?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "baffbb52-eb8f-41ee-8842-91b89ae393bb",
                     "text": "Bostoen, K.",
                     "page": "pp. 228",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c38ef469-13b7-4292-b8ab-69231fbfd1da",
                     "text": "Pakendorf, B., de Filippo, C., & Bostoen, K.",
                     "page": "pp. 63",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "5f55c3fc-d434-4d56-a6fc-838649b8aa97",
                     "text": "Yanovich, I.",
                     "page": "pp. 304",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a4f22e25-5839-407f-a1aa-634521d52b42",
                     "text": "González Redondo, Francisco A., et al.",
                     "page": "pp. 180 - 181",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-5000",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-4000",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The questions in this reflection still need deeper study and more data from Africa before we can answer them clearly. For now, the evidence is there. As Dr. Wilbert Z. Sadomba writes, the Shona mastered many kinds of knowledge that we would explain today with mathematics (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cc162709-bdc3-469b-a208-e900f4570842",
                     "text": "Sadomba, Zvakanyorwa Wilbert",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
